[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Understanding the Context Package and Cancellation in Go"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"In Go, the context package is a fundamental primitive for managing lifetimes of operations, signaling cancellation, and passing request-scoped values across API boundaries and between goroutines."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Why Do We Need Context Cancellation?"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"When building concurrent applications, you often spawn multiple goroutines to handle tasks like database queries, HTTP requests, or complex computations. If the parent operation times out or is aborted by the client, you want all descendant goroutines to stop working immediately to free up resources and avoid wasted CPU cycles."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Core Concepts of Context"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"context.Background(): Returns an empty context, usually used as the top-level parent in main functions, initialization, and tests."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"context.TODO(): Used when you are unsure which context to use or when the function is yet to be updated with a context parameter."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"context.WithCancel(parent): Returns a derived context and a cancel function. Calling the cancel function closes the Done() channel."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"context.WithTimeout(parent, duration): Automatically cancels the context after a specified duration elapses."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Basic Cancellation"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"package main

import (
	"context"
	"fmt"
	"time"
)

func worker(ctx context.Context, id int) {
	for {
		select {
		case <-ctx.Done():
			fmt.Printf("Worker %d: shutting down...\n", id)
			return
		default:
			fmt.Printf("Worker %d: working...\n", id)
			time.Sleep(500 * time.Millisecond)
		}
	}
}

func main() {
	ctx, cancel := context.WithTimeout(context.Background(), 2*time.Second)
	defer cancel()

	go worker(ctx, 1)

	time.Sleep(2 * time.Second)
	fmt.Println("Main: application exiting")
}"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Best Practices for Using Context"},{"order":13,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always pass context.Context as the first parameter to functions that require cancellation or timeouts, conventionally named 'ctx'."},{"order":14,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Do not store contexts inside a struct type; instead, pass it explicitly to each function that needs it."},{"order":15,"type":"bullet_point","text":"Always call the cancel function returned by WithCancel, WithTimeout, or WithDeadline to prevent memory leaks."}]
